Repro: 1. Create a project and switch to View > LaTeX. 2. At the end of the body, before \end{document}, add these lines without blank lines between the paragraph and the commands:
ZXTIGHT paragraph before a section
\section{ZXTIGHT SECTION}
ZXTIGHT section body
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
ZXTIGHT & 9 \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
3. Click 'Apply source', then View > Visual, then View > LaTeX.
Actual: Apply reports success (`Visual document synchronized from LaTeX.`), but the visual document has no ZXTIGHT SECTION heading and no table; it shows the literal text `\section{ZXTIGHT SECTION}`, `\begin{table}`, `\begin{tabular}` and `\end{tabular}`. Returning to the source shows the commands rewritten as `\textbackslash{}section\{ZXTIGHT SECTION\}`, `\textbackslash{}begin\{table\}`, etc., so the LaTeX is permanently corrupted as well. The same failure occurs for a \section command alone without the table, so any block command that is not preceded by a blank line is affected.

Repro: 1. Choose 'New document', open View > 'LaTeX', and insert this valid LaTeX before `\end{document}`:
\begin{quote}
\begin{equation*}
q=1
\end{equation*}
\end{quote}
2. Click 'Apply source' and switch to View > 'Visual'.
3. Open View > 'LaTeX' and click 'Refresh from visual', then read document.tex.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 with this list version instead: `\begin{itemize}`, `\item Before`, `\begin{equation*}`, `L=1`, `\end{equation*}`, `\end{itemize}` (one per line).
Expected: as with a top-level equation, the display equation is imported as an editable `mathBlock` and re-exported unchanged inside the quote/list (top-level `\begin{equation}...\end{equation}` does exactly this).
Actual: the quote shows the literal text `\begin{equation*} q=1 \end{equation*}` with no math node (`math: 0`), and the list item shows `Before \begin{equation*} L=1 \end{equation*}`; the canonical export escapes them to `\textbackslash{}begin\{equation*\}` ... `\textbackslash{}end\{equation*\}`, so the PDF would print the environment as text and the equation is lost from the document.
Actual: None

Repro: Create a new document. Type 'outer', click Home > Bulleted list, press Enter, type 'child one', press Tab, press Enter, type 'child two', press Enter, type 'child three'. Click at the end of 'child one', press Enter (an empty second child item appears), press Enter again and type 'typed'. Repeat with Home > Numbered list.
Actual: 'typed' is inserted inside the parent list item, between two split child lists: the DOM is '<ul><li><p>outer</p><ul><li><p>child one</p></li></ul><p>typed</p><ul><li><p>child two</p></li><li><p>child three</p></li></ul></li></ul>' and the LaTeX is '\item outer
 egin{itemize}
\item child one
\end{itemize}
typed
 egin{itemize}
\item child two
\item child three
\end{itemize}', so 'typed' becomes text of the parent item rather than a list item. The numbered variant behaves the same and exports the second nested list as '\setcounter{enumii}{1}'.

Repro: Create a new document. Type 'outer', click Home > Bulleted list, press Enter, type 'inner one', press Tab, press Enter, type 'inner two'. Click at the end of 'inner one' and click Home > Bulleted list (the button is shown pressed). Open Source in the status bar, click Refresh from visual, and read the LaTeX textarea.
Actual: The editor DOM is '<ul><li><p>outer</p><p>inner one</p><ul><li><p>inner two</p></li></ul></li></ul>' (two paragraph blocks), but the generated LaTeX is ' egin{itemize}
\item outer
inner one
 egin{itemize}
\item inner two
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}'. The two paragraphs are joined by a single newline, which LaTeX reads as a space, so 'outer' and 'inner one' print as one paragraph in the PDF.

Repro: Create a new document and type '[draft] note'. Click Home > Bulleted list, open Source in the status bar, click Refresh from visual and read the LaTeX textarea. Clear the document, type '[appendix] item', click Home > Numbered list and read the LaTeX again.
Actual: The LaTeX is ' egin{itemize}
\item [draft] note
\end{itemize}', and the numbered variant is ' egin{enumerate}
\item [appendix] item
\end{enumerate}'. Text in brackets directly after \item is LaTeX's optional label argument, so it replaces the bullet/number and the literal brackets are dropped; the PDF prints 'draft note' with 'draft' as the label instead of '[draft] note'.

Repro: Create a new document. Type 'outer', click Home > Bulleted list, press Enter, type 'child one', press Tab, press Enter, type 'child two', press Enter, type 'child three'. Click at the end of 'child two' and press Shift+Tab. Open Source in the status bar, click Refresh from visual and read the LaTeX textarea.
Actual: The outdented item adopts the following sibling. After outdenting 'child two' the DOM is '<ul><li>outer<ul>child one</ul></li><li>child two<ul>child three</ul></li></ul>' and the LaTeX is ' egin{itemize}
\item outer
 egin{itemize}
\item child one
\end{itemize}
\item child two
 egin{itemize}
\item child three
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}', so 'child three' becomes a child of 'child two' visually and in the PDF. Outdenting 'child one' instead moves both child two and child three under it. The same thing happens with a numbered sublist and when Enter exits an empty nested item in the middle of a sublist (probe scenarios outdentMiddleNumberedNested and nestedEmptyMiddle).

Repro: Open the demo project. View > LaTeX, append the following before \end{document}: ' egin{verbatim}
CODE LINE ONE
CODE LINE TWO
\end{verbatim}

AFTER CODE', then click Apply source and View > Visual. Click the code text, press Home, then ArrowRight four times so the caret is after 'CODE'. Insert > Image and choose any PNG. The PRE block now contains only 'CODE'; a new normal paragraph ' LINE ONE CODE LINE TWO' (containing one inline 120x60 img plus one 0x0 img) appears between the code block and 'AFTER CODE'. View > LaTeX shows ' egin{verbatim}
CODE
\end{verbatim}' followed by '\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=1.5em,keepaspectratio]{assets/images/code-probe.png}% Sentence alt: code probe.png
 LINE ONE
CODE LINE TWO'. No toast, no console error. Probe qa/probes/insert-media.mjs scenario 'image-in-code-block'.
Actual: The inline image insertion splits the text-only code block: the verbatim body is cut at the caret ('CODE'), the remainder ' LINE ONE CODE LINE TWO' is re-exported and displayed as ordinary prose outside  egin{verbatim}, and the image is spliced into that prose. The visible defect is a silent, immediate loss of the formatting and position of most of the code block.

Repro: 1. Create a project and open View > LaTeX.
2. Replace the body before \end{document} with:
\begin{center}
\begin{equation}
E=mc^2
\end{equation}
\end{center}

\begin{flushleft}
\[
F=ma
\]
\end{flushleft}
3. Click Apply source. The toast says "Visual document synchronized from LaTeX."; switch to View > Visual.
4. Return to View > LaTeX and click Refresh from visual.
Actual: Zero math nodes are created. The visual page shows literal paragraphs `e̱gin{equation} E=mc^2 \end{equation}` and `\[ F=ma \]`; Refresh from visual rewrites the body to `e̱gin\{equation\}`, `E=mc\textasciicircum{}2`, `\textbackslash{}end\{equation\}`, and escapes the flushleft delimiters as `\textbackslash{}[` and `\textbackslash{}]`. Applying the export again is reported as a success and reproduces the same escaped text, so the original valid source is lost.

Repro: 1. Create a project and open View > LaTeX.
2. Replace the body before \end{document} with:
\begin{quote}
Quoted text

\begin{align}
a &= b \\
c &= d
\end{align}
\end{quote}

\begin{itemize}
\item Item text

\begin{gather}
g=1 \\
h=2
\end{gather}
\end{itemize}
3. Click Apply source (the toast reports success), switch to View > Visual, then go back to View > LaTeX and click Refresh from visual.
Actual: Zero math nodes are created. The quote shows `Quoted text e̱gin{align} a &= b c &= d \end{align}` and the list item shows `Item texte̱gin{gather} g=1 h=2 \end{gather}`. Refresh from visual writes `e̱gin\{align\}`, `a \&= b`, `\textbackslash{}end\{align\}`, and the same for gather; a second Apply reproduces the escaped text. The plain equation and $$ forms in the same containers do work, so only the hand-written align/gather forms are missed.
